  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A fall-like feel with seasonable temperatures\nand very low humidity today, becoming more summer-like Sunday.\nThe weekend remains dry, with increasing clouds late Sunday.\n\nHigh pressure in control for most of this weekend. Dry weather\nprevails for most of this weekend. Noticeably lower humidity and\ntemperatures as well today. As this high pressure moves offshore\nSunday, southwest winds will start to increase temperatures and\nhumidity once again.\n\nThe latest guidance suite, particularly the NAM and GFS, have \ntrended a little wetter towards the south coast of New England \nlate Sunday. This is most likely due to a projected weak surface\ntrough associate with a weak low pressure approaching from the \nwest. Introduced a low risk for some showers late in the day,\nbut these are more likely to arrive Sunday night when this low\npressure should make its closest approach.\n\nAdditionally, there is a moderate risk for rip currents today\ntowards the ocean-exposed Cape beaches and Nantucket. Those \nheading to the beach should check with beach staff or lifeguards\nregarding rip current conditions before entering the water. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cloudy and unsettled early to mid next week \nwith several opportunities for showers and storms. While Monday \nnow looks cloudy but generally dry, better chances for showers \nand storms are anticipated for Tue through Thurs.\n\nTiming and location differences continue to muddy the waters\nwith the latest guidance suite. Some members keep a backdoor\ncold front across southern New England. Meanwhile, others have \na more dominant high pressure over eastern Canada, which nudges\nthis front just far enough south to keep Monday dry. Based on\nsome trends in the ensemble guidance, continuing to favor a dry\nday for Monday, with a little more hope for more sunshine than\nprevious forecasts.\n\nThis is not to say that this front goes far from our region. The\nexpectation is that it will be more of a factor in our weather\nTuesday into Wednesday. Another cold front associated with a low\npressure over eastern Canada should then take over heading into\nThursday. Still looking at periods of showers and storms during\nthis time. Expect a narrower range in high/low temperatures, \nwith highs a little below normal and lows slightly above normal.",
